      <h2 id="vault-questions">9. Important pole-vault-specific questions most recruiting guides miss</h2>
      <p>Ask:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Does the school own a complete pole series for my current and projected body weight / grip?</li>
        <li>Who pays for replacement poles?</li>
        <li>Can athletes travel with poles on team flights, and how does the program handle pole shipping?</li>
        <li>Is there an indoor runway/pit?</li>
        <li>Can I vault year-round?</li>
        <li>Does the pole vault coach travel to most competitions?</li>
        <li>Does the program recruit vaulters every year or only when roster spots open?</li>
        <li>What is the coach-to-vaulter ratio?</li>
        <li>Does the school support post-collegiate vaulting?</li>
        <li>If I am a multi-event athlete, will I be developed primarily as a vaulter, multi, or both?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="red-flags">10. Red flags</h2>
      <p>Be cautious when:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Nobody can clearly tell you who will coach the vault.</li>
        <li>The staff directory and what you are being told do not match.</li>
        <li>A coach guarantees future scholarship increases verbally but will not explain the written aid process.</li>
        <li>A school pressures you to commit before you understand admissions or the financial package.</li>
        <li>The program has no suitable poles and expects you to provide an entire series without discussing cost.</li>
        <li>You are told not to speak with current athletes.</li>
        <li>The academic program you want cannot realistically coexist with practice/travel and nobody will address it.</li>
        <li>The school quotes an old NCAA rule when you ask about current scholarship, roster or eligibility rules.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="final-advice">11. Final AZPVA advice</h2>
      <p>Recruiting is proactive. Do not wait for coaches to find you.</p>
      <p>Your goal is not simply to attend the highest NCAA division possible. Your goal is to find a school where:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>You can afford to graduate.</li>
        <li>You can study what you want.</li>
        <li>You have a coach who understands your event.</li>
        <li>You have access to the equipment and facilities needed to improve.</li>
        <li>You will have a realistic opportunity to compete.</li>
        <li>You like the people and would still want to attend if track disappeared tomorrow.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Cast a wide net. A great fit can exist in DI, DII, DIII, NAIA or junior college.</p>

